WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.911
During incidents, telemetry helps responders reconstruct events, identify affected systems and accounts, understand behavior, determine scope, and support containment and recovery decisions.

2
00:00:12.911 --> 00:00:18.588
Evidence from multiple sources can establish what happened before, during, and after an alert.

3
00:00:18.588 --> 00:00:25.812
Responders often build timelines by correlating identity, endpoint, network, application, and cloud events.

4
00:00:25.812 --> 00:00:29.095
Consistent timestamps and identifiers are essential.

5
00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:38.384
A device name, account, IP address, session, request ID, or cloud resource may connect records that would otherwise appear unrelated.

6
00:00:38.384 --> 00:00:41.827
Retention and accessibility directly affect response.

7
00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:50.547
If important events have expired, were never enabled, or require a slow manual request, teams may be unable to answer urgent questions.

8
00:00:50.547 --> 00:00:58.908
Incident planning should identify critical sources, access procedures, evidence preservation needs, and fallback options before a crisis.

9
00:00:58.908 --> 00:01:01.682
Investigations also improve telemetry.

10
00:01:01.682 --> 00:01:10.213
Lessons learned may reveal missing event types, unclear fields, collection failures, or context that would have accelerated analysis.

11
00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:16.529
Feeding those findings into engineering work makes future detection and response more efficient and defensible.

12
00:01:16.529 --> 00:01:20.041
Telemetry used as incident evidence should be handled carefully.

13
00:01:20.041 --> 00:01:29.460
Access controls, integrity protections, documented exports, case references, and consistent time handling help teams preserve confidence in the record.

14
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:37.901
Response procedures should identify who can retrieve sensitive sources and how evidence will be retained when normal platform retention is too short.
